{:edb/db-before-t 1847 :edb/db-after-t 1848 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535160 50 #inst "2026-10-13T01:54:11.907Z" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072675 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072676 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072677 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072678 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072679 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476318 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476334 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476573 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476582 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072674 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476595 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072674 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476573 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186072842 1078 #edb/ref 17592186072847 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186073539 1074 "To find the fraction of the original bag Jim received, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nby\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{7} = \\dfrac {1 \\times 6} {3 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{6}{21}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{21} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{21 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, Jim received\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nof the initial bag of marbles." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186073539 1074 "To find the fraction of the original bag Jim received, we need to multiply $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ by $\\dfrac{6}{7}.$  \n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{6}{7} =  \\dfrac {1 \\times 6} {3 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{6}{21}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{21} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{21 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, Jim received $\\dfrac{2}{7}$ of the initial bag of marbles." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186073540 1074 "To find out the amount of coffee Jacob drank, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 8}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{8}{12}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{12} = \\dfrac{8 \\div 4}{12\\div 4} =\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Jacob drank\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ncups of coffee." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186073540 1074 "To find out the amount of coffee Jacob drank, we need to calculate $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3}.$\n\nTo do that, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3} =  \\dfrac{1 \\times 8}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{8}{12}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{12} = \\dfrac{8 \\div 4}{12\\div 4} =\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Jacob drank $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ cups of coffee." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186073860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476946 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186073861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476883 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186073861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476930 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186073985 1050 420.0 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186074406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186074406 1071 "A bag contained $\\frac{9}{7}\\,\\text{kg}$ of soil. Annie poured $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the bag into a pot. How many kilograms of soil did Annie pour into the pot?" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186074406 1071 "A bag contained $\\frac{9}{7}kg$ of soil. Annie poured $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the bag into a pot. How many kilograms of soil did Annie pour into the pot?" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186074406 1074 "To find out the weight of soil that Annie poured, we need to calculate $\\frac{9}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTo multiply the two numbers, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{9\\times 1}{7\\times 3}=\\frac{9}{21}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{21}=\\frac{9÷3}{21÷3}=\\frac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, Annie poured $\\frac{3}{7}\\,\\text{kg}$ of soil into the pot." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186074434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186074434 1074 "To find out the fraction of the students in the class who are girls that like to play soccer, we need to multiply $\\frac{3}{5}$ by $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nWe multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3\\times 1}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{3}{10}$ of all students in the class are girls who like to play soccer." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186074958 1064 "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\\,\\text{kg}\n$$\n\nof flour. If Nancy used\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nof the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nby\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{5\\times 5} = \\dfrac{6}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Nancy used\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{25}\n$$\n\nkilograms of flour." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186074958 1064 "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains ![](images/bc/bcc2ab102a29294d71449d99af1cf1a88aae88047223f829175086f7a6cd5e10.svg) of flour. If Nancy used ![](images/c0/c0db422914dd04be9adf05af649ec06419e80d8425cd0a85f6105cef7cc447b5.svg) of the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply ![](images/16/1627bb3010ada55ed941966d38081f1a6c1394c07ca78b94585f8c97e4bbc138.svg) by ![](images/d9/d989525f1462386565e344f7e9a41d48cea1e85148f9f5aba25268f589a8bcbc.svg)\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n![](images/58/58824e95f00448b5c7d509f0781a367506ccf37fa018f2a0ba373c74191f1bb6.svg)\n\nTherefore, Nancy used ![](images/fd/fd1a8efcb6ae625f2259a43e8ffeb93d486d409af6175b6e52380f4ccd488af7.svg) kilograms of flour." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119043 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119044 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119045 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119046 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119047 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476219 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476263 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476277 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476400 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476544 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119042 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476400 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119529 1071 "Simplify the polynomial $x^3+x-2x^2-3x$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119529 1071 "Simplify the polynomial $x^3+x-2x^2-3x.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119529 1074 "Here, $x$ and $-3x$ are like terms, so we group them, and then we add or subtract the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + x - 2x^{2} - 3x &=  \\\\\nx^{3} + x - 3x - 2x^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{3} + (x - 3x) - 2x^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{3} + (1 - 3)x - 2x^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3} - 2x - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119529 1074 "Here, $x$ and $-3x$ are like terms, so we group them, and then we add or subtract the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x-2{x}^{2}-3x & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+x-3x-2{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+(x-3x)-2{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+(1-3)x-2{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}-2x-2{x}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119530 1071 "Rewrite the polynomial below in standard form.\n\n$8x-9-3x^4 - 6x^3$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119530 1071 "Rewrite the polynomial below in standard form. \n\n$$\n8x-9-3x^4 - 6x^3\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119530 1074 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms in descending order of the variable's exponent. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nThe highest exponent term is $3x^4$, followed by $-6x^3$, and then $8x$, and finally $-9$.\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is\n$-3x^4 - 6x^3 + 8x-9$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119530 1074 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms in descending order of the variable's exponent. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nThe highest exponent term is $3x^4,$ followed by $-6x^3,$ and then $8x,$ and finally $-9.$\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n-3x^4 - 6x^3 + 8x-9.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119531 1071 "Simplify the polynomial $3x^2-2-4x - 6x^2+18$, giving your final answer in standard form." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119531 1071 "Simplify the polynomial $3x^2-2-4x - 6x^2+18,$ giving your final answer in standard form." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119531 1074 "First, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 2 - 4x - 6x^{2} + 18 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 6x^{2} - 2 + 18 - 4x &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 6x^{2}) + (-2 + 18) - 4x &=  \\\\\n(3 - 6)x^{2} + (-2 + 18) - 4x &=  \\\\\n&= - 3x^{2} + 16 - 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to express our polynomial in standard form.\n\nThe highest exponent term is $-3x^2$, followed by $-4x$, and finally $16$.\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n-3x^2-4x+16\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186119531 1074 "First, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-2-4x-6{x}^{2}+18 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}-2+18-4x & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2})+(-2+18)-4x & = \\\\ (3-6){x}^{2}+(-2+18)-4x & = \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+16-4x & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to express our polynomial in standard form. \n\nThe highest exponent term is $-3x^2,$ followed by $-4x,$ and finally $16.$\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n-3x^2-4x+16.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476872 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476933 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476922 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476964 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476972 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476970 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186119841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476957 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186120005 1050 420.0 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186120488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186120488 1071 "The standard form of the polynomial $2 + 3x^{2} + 4x^{3} - x^{2} - x^{3}$ is" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186120488 1071 "The standard form of the polynomial $2+3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ is" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186120488 1074 "First, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & = \\\\ 2+(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2})+(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ 2+(3-1){x}^{2}+(4-1){x}^{3} & = \\\\ 2+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to express our polynomial in standard form.\n\nThe highest exponent term is $3{x}^{3},$ followed by $2{x}^{2},$ and finally $2.$\n\nSo, the standard form of the polynomial is:\n\n$$\n3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+2\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186121146 1064 "We can simplify the polynomial below because it contains a pair of like terms:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3x^2}} +4x \\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,{\\color{blue}{2x^2}} +5\n$$\n\nTo simplify, we combine the like terms ($3x^2$ and $-2x^2$) by adding or subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x-2{x}^{2}+5 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2})+4x+5 & = \\\\ (3-2){x}^{2}+4x+5 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186121146 1064 "We can simplify the polynomial below because it contains a pair of like terms:\n${\\color{blue}{3x^2}} +4x \\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,{\\color{blue}{2x^2}} +5$\n\nTo simplify, we combine the like terms ($3x^2$ and $-2x^2$) by adding or subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} + 4x - 2x^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 2x^{2} + 4x + 5 &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 2x^{2}) + 4x + 5 &=  \\\\\n(3 - 2)x^{2} + 4x + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 4x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186121147 1064 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms of the variable's exponent in descending order. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nFor example, consider the following polynomial:\n\n$$\n6x-2x^2-2+x^3\n$$\n\nTo write this polynomial in standard form, we follow the steps below:\n\n1\\. Identify the term with the highest exponent ($x^3$) and write it first.\n\n2\\. Identify the term with the next highest exponent ($-2x^2$) and write it second.\n\n3\\. Continue identifying the term with the next highest exponent and writing it next until all of the polynomial terms have been written.\n\nTherefore, the standard form of our polynomial is\n\n$$\nx^3 -2x^2 + 6x -2.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The leading term always appears first when a polynomial is written in standard form. This is where the leading term gets its name!\n\n- When a polynomial is written in standard form, we also say that the terms are written in **decreasing powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** or **descending powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** because the powers are decreasing/descending.\n\n- If we were to write the terms of our polynomial in *increasing* (or *ascending*) powers of $x,$ it would look as follows:\n\n$$\n-2 + 6x -2x^2 + x^3\n$$\n\nIn this case, the powers of $x$ *increase* as we read from left to right." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186121147 1064 "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms of the variable's exponent in descending order. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nFor example, consider the following polynomial:\n$6x-2x^2-2+x^3$\n\nTo write this polynomial in standard form, we follow the steps below:\n\n1. Identify the term with the highest exponent ($x^3$) and write it first.\n2. Identify the term with the next highest exponent ($-2x^2$) and write it second.\n3. Continue identifying the term with the next highest exponent and writing it next until all of the polynomial terms have been written.\n\nTherefore, the standard form of our polynomial is\n$x^3 -2x^2 + 6x -2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The leading term always appears first when a polynomial is written in standard form. This is where the leading term gets its name!\n- When a polynomial is written in standard form, we also say that the terms are written in **decreasing powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** or **descending powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** because the powers are decreasing/descending.\n- If we were to write the terms of our polynomial in *increasing* (or *ascending*) powers of $x$, it would look as follows:\n$-2 + 6x -2x^2 + x^3$\nIn this case, the powers of $x$ *increase* as we read from left to right." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186123830 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123833 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124294 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{12}$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186124294 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{12}.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124294 1074 "Note that\n\n$$\n12=4 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhere $4$ is a perfect square. So, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{12} =\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 3}= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186124294 1074 "Note that $12=4 \\cdot 3,$ where $4$ is a perfect square. So, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{12} =\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 3}= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124295 1074 "Note that\n\n$$\n3 = \\sqrt{9}\n$$\n\nSo, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{9}\\cdot\\sqrt{5}= \\sqrt{9\\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{45}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186124295 1074 "Note that $3 = \\sqrt{9}.$ So, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{9}\\cdot\\sqrt{5}= \\sqrt{9\\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{45}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124296 1074 "Using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot\\sqrt[3]{3}= \\sqrt[3]{2\\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{6}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186124296 1074 "Using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot\\sqrt[3]{3}= \\sqrt[3]{2\\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{6}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124481 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476915 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124482 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476886 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124482 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476944 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124483 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476873 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124483 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476923 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124587 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476898 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186124720 1050 420.0 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186125205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186125205 1071 "$\\sqrt{50}=$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186125205 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{50}$?" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186125205 1074 "The square root of a product of two positive numbers is equal to the product of the square roots of the numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{50}=\\sqrt{25\\cdot 2}=\\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186125769 1064 "To multiply square roots, we simply multiply the numbers under the root. This is called the **product rule for radicals**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}$ by applying the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nThe product rule also works for cube roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = \\sqrt[3]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{10}\n$$\n\nIn general, the product rule works for any product of $n$th roots. So for any $n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{2} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{5} = \\sqrt[n]{2 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[n]{10}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot use the product rule when we have the product of different radicals, such as square roots and cube roots. For example, the expression $\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt[3]{5}$ cannot be simplified because the radicals $\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ and $\