 and we want to convert it to the square root of a single number, then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n7 \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{49 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{98}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186125771 1064 "The product rule applies to cube roots as well.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt[3]{24}$. Note that\n\n$$\n24=8 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhere $8$ is a perfect cube. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{24} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = 2 \\sqrt[3]{3}\n$$\n\nAgain, if we're given a simplified cube root and we want to convert it to the cube root of a single number then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n2 \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{24}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186125771 1064 "The product rule applies to cube roots as well.\n\nFor example, consider $\\sqrt[3]{24}.$ Note that $24=8 \\cdot 3,$ where $8$ is a perfect cube. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{24} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = 2 \\sqrt[3]{3}.\n$$\n\nAgain, if we're given a simplified cube root and we want to convert it to the cube root of a single number then we can work backwards using the product rule:\n\n$$\n2 \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{3} = \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{24}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186128933 1071 "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+8x-5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, what is $\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}?$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186128933 1071 "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, what is $\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}?$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186128933 1074 "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+8x-5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $2x^2+8x -5$ by the linear binomial ${\\color{purple}x+5}$ gives\n\n- the quotient ${\\color{blue}2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5} = {\\color{blue}2x-2}+\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{purple}x+5}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186128933 1074 "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $2x^2+8x -5$ by the linear binomial ${\\color{purple}x+5}$ gives\n\n- the quotient ${\\color{blue}2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5} = {\\color{blue}2x-2}+\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{purple}x+5}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129117 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476881 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129117 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476896 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129117 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476899 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129117 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476938 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129117 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476955 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476894 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476941 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476961 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476971 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476880 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476900 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476910 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476921 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476935 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129356 1050 600.0 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129607 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129607 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186129607 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186129607 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186130415 1064 "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6efe303dc4179f60a6a68f30f30314819dae8510f23ce4bc60a9f88613d1d07c.svg)\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n![](images/f7/f76a89552aaabe7e248cf2af36be8e2fdd29bcdd8c2a7d7294a143cc92af377a.svg)\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n![](images/ff/ffcf7ee728fd73358692efedd018102f85d7434a7519ba3d14b7e5b3c0c39287.svg)\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n![](images/30/30c74da88f05426a4360e2f9c5b944d69003e5b32f590ef0ac861649dee743b8.svg)\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n![](images/24/24b68faa2a799d11f48914401d37e2fcaa8f27b313c67284eb1fef604425d7b5.svg)\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n![](images/e0/e02317fccf7968dbc3278665dd1145585139503d88804e46bdc76bb899cc1201.svg)\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$\n![](images/66/6610a2ad32f8d00a76965fc27924546c77f371de1d65061640a9230857bb37f4.svg)\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186130415 1064 "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186132595 1075 #edb/ref 1759218613