rt{\\phantom{x}}$) and obtain a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16}=\\sqrt{4^2}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{8}$ only." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186133501 1074 "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126}=11.224\\,972\\,160\\,..\n$$\n\nNow, we round to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126} \\approx 11.225\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186133501 1074 "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126}=11.224\\,972\\,160\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126} \\approx 11.225\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186133502 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{11}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186133502 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{2},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{11}$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186133502 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$, cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}}$, or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{\\phantom{0}}, \\sqrt[5]{\\phantom{0}},\\ldots$, etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1.259\\,921\\,\\ldots$\n- $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $27$ are perfect cubes, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}}|= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1^{3}}{3^{3}}}; = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}}; = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}}; = \\sqrt[3]{(\\frac{1}{3})^{3}}; = \\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{11} = 2.223\\,980\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ only." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186133502 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$, cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{\\phantom{0}}, \\sqrt[5]{\\phantom{0}},\\ldots,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1.259\\,921\\,\\ldots$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $27$ are perfect cubes, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{11} = 2.223\\,980\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ only." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186133515 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186133515 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186133515 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}} = 0.645\\,497\\,\\ldots$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8\\cdot 8}{9\\cdot 9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{8}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}} = 1.195\\,228\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ only." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186133515 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}} = 0.645\\,497\\,\\ldots$\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n$\\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}}|= \\sqrt{\\frac{8^{2}}{9^{2}}}; = \\sqrt{\\frac{8 \\cdot 8}{9 \\cdot 9}}; = \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9} \\cdot \\frac{8}{9}}; = \\sqrt{(\\frac{8}{9})^{2}}; = \\frac{8}{9}$\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}} = 1.195\\,228\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}\n$$\n\nonly." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186133563 1071 "The linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ has the standard matrix \n![](images/34/343c9e46cf59cf4372a563c80dd9010bcece8c04f8babdb95d57c04cdf4a2503.png) \nWhat are the images of the s