ae41f19ca495de5.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1/2}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186162302 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X>0.5,Y>0.5}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/7e/7e81ce16404bc6c5c826f79a88519bd6216156f5bb0f8af1eae41f19ca495de5.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1/2}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186162313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186162313 1071 "Find $E[XY^{2}]$, given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {1,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186162313 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186162313 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186162314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186162314 1071 "Find $E[\\sin (X + Y)]$, given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{8}{π^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}, 0 \\le y \\le x, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186162314 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186162314 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/a4/a4f7e4e3f5e6a46fdc1b61a40986aec7b47fe75b37c8f042497aa0f3a0969f30.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186163330 1064 "Let's show that the following function is a valid joint PDF:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe only need to check the first two conditions to show that this is a valid joint PDF for *some* pair of random variables. So let's do that:\n\n- Clearly $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ so the first condition is satisfied. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- To check the second condition, let's first define a region $D$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq   1,\\,0 \\leq y\\leq   1\\}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this region is shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nLet's now integrate $f(x,y)$ over $\\mathbb R^2.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second condition is satisfied too.\n\nHence, $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function.\n\nWe'll learn how to compute probabilities using the joint PDF later in this lesson." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186163330 1064 "Let's show that the following function is a valid joint PDF:\n![](images/2f/2fcc03e45f23018485c2aff1ff3c6804899928e37bedaa05d9dafc5e02780179.svg)\n\nWe only need to check the first two conditions to show that this is a valid joint PDF for *some* pair of random variables. So let's do that:\n\n- Clearly $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ so the first condition is satisfied. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- To check the second condition, let's first define a region $D$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq   1,\\,0 \\leq y\\leq   1\\}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this region is shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nLet's now integrate $f(x,y)$ over $\\mathbb R^2.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second condition is satisfied too.\n\nHence, $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function.\n\nWe'll learn how to compute probabilities using the joint PDF later in this lesson." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186163335 1064 "Recall that if $X$ is a discrete random variable with support $S_X$, and $Y = r(X)$ for some function $r$, then we can compute the expected value of $Y$ using the rule of the lazy statistician, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x)\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $W = r(X,Y)$ is a function of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. Analogously to the case above, the **rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables** states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[W] =\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)\\big]=\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y) \\, f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is a helpful rule for two reasons:\n\n- Firstly, it allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[W]$ without needing to find the PMF of $W$. This turns out to be very useful indeed.\n- Secondly, we can use this rule to prove many useful properties of random variables. For example, it can be used to prove that\n$\\textrm E[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm E[X] \\pm \\textrm E[Y]$.\nWe'll prove some useful results using our rule at the end of this lesson." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186163335 1064 "Recall that if $X$ is a discrete random variable with support $S_X,$ and $Y = r(X)$ for some function $r,$ then we can compute the expected value of $Y$ using the rule of the lazy statistician, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] =  \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x).\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $W = r(X,Y)$ is a function of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. Analogously to the case above, the **rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables** states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[W] =\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)\\big]=\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y) \\, f(x,y),\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is a helpful rule for two reasons:\n\n- Firstly, it allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[W]$ without needing to find the PMF of $W.$ This turns out to be very useful indeed.\n\n- Secondly, we can use this rule to prove many useful properties of random variables. For example, it can be used to prove that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm E[X] \\pm \\textrm E[Y].\n$$\n\nWe'll prove some useful results using our rule at the end of this lesson." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186163336 1064 "Let's prove that for continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X+Y] = \\textrm{E}[X] + \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nr(x, y) = x+y\n$$\n\nBy the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X + Y] &= E[r(X, Y)] \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})(x + y)f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})xf(x, y)dxdy + ∬_(R^{2})yf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf(x, y)dxdy + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the integration domain is rectangular, we can swap the order of integration in the first integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X + Y] &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf(x, y)dydx + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}x\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dydx + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf_{X}(x)dx + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf_{Y}(y)dy \\\\\n&= E[X] + E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. Note that we made use of the fact that\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y, \\qquad f_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nThe proof for discrete random variables is similar." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186163336 1064 "Let's prove that for continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X+Y] = \\textrm{E}[X] + \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nLet $r(x, y) = x+y.$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[r(X,Y)] \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(x+y)f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y+{∬}_{{R}^{2}}yf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }xf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }yf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the integration domain is rectangular, we can swap the order of integration in the first integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }xf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }yf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. Note that we made use of the fact that\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty  f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y, \\qquad f_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty  f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThe proof for discrete random variables is similar." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186163337 1064 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)] = \\iint_{\\mathbb{R}^2} r(x,y) \\, f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is especially useful for continuous random variables since computing the PDF of $W = r(X, Y)$ can be challenging." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186163337 1064 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)] = \\iint_{\\mathbb{R}^2} r(x,y) \\, f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is especially useful for continuous random variables since computing the PDF of $W = r(X, Y)$ can be challenging." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186163342 1064 "Let's prove that for continuous *independent* random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[XY] = \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nr(x, y) = xy\n$$\n\nBy the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= E[r(X, Y)] \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})xyf(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ and $Y$ are independent by assumption, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y(y)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∬_(R^{2})xyf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})xyf_{X}(x)f_{Y}(y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xyf_{X}(x)f_{Y}(y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf_{X}(x)dx \\cdot \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf_{Y}(y)dy \\\\\n&= E[X] \\cdot E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proof for discrete random variables is similar." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186163342 1064 "Let's prove that for continuous *independent* random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[XY] = \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nLet $r(x, y) = xy.$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & =\\text{E}[r(X,Y)] \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ and $Y$ are independent by assumption, we have $f(x,y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y(y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy{f}_{X}(x){f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }xy{f}_{X}(x){f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proof for discrete random variables is similar." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166232 1078 #edb/ref 17592186166237 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476943 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476918 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476936 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476976 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476890 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476887 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476891 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186167504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186167504 1071 "In a game show, a contestant can choose a prize from any box. The boxes are labeled with numbers $5$ through $15$. If the contestant chooses a box at random, what is the probability that the box number they pick is less than $10$?" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186167504 1071 "In a game show, a contestant can choose a prize from any box. The boxes are labeled with numbers $5$ through $15.$ If the contestant chooses a box at random, what is the probability that the box number they pick is less than $10?$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186167504 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen box. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,15\\}$ has $|S|=15-5+1=11$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11}, & x=5,6,…,15 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<10$ are $5,6,7,8,9.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}=\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186173806 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173807 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186173806 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173808 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186173806 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173809 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186173806 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173810 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186173806 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173811 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186173806 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476226 131