074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\nf_{X}(x) = \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dy, f_{Y}(y) = \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dx\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: y^2 \\leq x \\leq1,\\, y\\geq 0 \\}\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c689a61af43d1d7aef57364a0d231256329305438e3fa046b048b7580fc13fe.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\int_{y^{2}}^{1}5y\\sqrt{x}dx \\\\\n&= 5y\\int_{y^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{x}dx \\\\\n&= 5y[\\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}]_{x = y^{2}}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10y}{3}[x^{3/2}]_{x = y^{2}}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10y}{3}[1^{3/2} - (y^{2})^{3/2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{10y}{3}[1 - y^{3}] \\\\\n&= (10(y - y^{4}))/(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{(10(y - y^{4}))/(3),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186175684 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\{(x,y) \\::\\:   y^2 \\leq x \\leq1,\\, y\\geq 0 \\}.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c689a61af43d1d7aef57364a0d231256329305438e3fa046b048b7580fc13fe.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}5y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5y{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5y{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10y}{3}{[{x}^{3/2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10y}{3}[{1}^{3/2}-{({y}^{2})}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{10y}{3}[1-{y}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{10(y-{y}^{4})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n![](images/45/455169bc586c4c80c170a1ec5b8a831814f60e378ae56f471c0db75f439f6c36.svg)" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186175852 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n![](images/95/95ded6d2fba38750193e0b88b6c957dd841203590c6ce6ad493b19c31c76ac0b.svg)\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF $f_Y(y).$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186175852 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{15}{7}x^{2}y^{4},\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF $f_Y(y)$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186175852 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\nf_{X}(x) = \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dy, f_{Y}(y) = \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dx\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq y \\leq1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{15}{7}x^{2}y^{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{7}y^{4}[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{x = 1}^{x = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{7}y^{4}[x^{3}]_{x = 1}^{x = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{7}y^{4}(2^{3} - 1^{3}) \\\\\n&= 5y^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{5y^{4},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186175852 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{a