c{3}{8}{[\\frac{1}{3}(x+y)^{3}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[(x+y)^{3}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[(1+y)^{3}-(-1+y)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[(y+1)^{3}-(y-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[({y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1)-({y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[(\\cancel{{y}^{3}}+3{y}^{2}+\\cancel{3y}+1)-(\\cancel{{y}^{3}}-3{y}^{2}+\\cancel{3y}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[2(3{y}^{2}+1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3{y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $Y$ for $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{f}_{Y}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{y}\\frac{1}{4}(3{u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{y}(3{u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[{u}^{3}+u]}_{-1}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{y}^{3}+y-(-1)^{3}-(-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{y}^{3}+y+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+y+2}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the marginal CDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/0a/0a44179f2169e2c2660c6bb6e5bb23d5298786ee5d3f0fb181c3b71bf61ca0fe.svg)" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186175999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476911 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186175999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476913 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186175999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476931 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186175999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476940 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476885 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476909 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476942 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476954 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476875 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476884 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476889 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476914 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476948 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176037 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476947 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176153 1050 1140.0 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176258 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176258 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{8}{3}x^{3}y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF $f_{Y}(y)$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186176258 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $1\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y[{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y({1}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & 1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176261 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0.2<X<1).$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176261 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{3}{2}(x^{2} + y^{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nFind $P(0.2 < X < 1)$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186176261 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2<X<1) & ={\\int }_{0.2}^{1}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0.2}^{1}(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}x]}_{0.2}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)]-[\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)] \\\\  & =1-0.104 \\\\  & =0.896.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176364 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\pi {x}^{2}},\\,{y}^{2}+1\\le x.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186176364 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with a joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = \\frac{1}{πx^{2}}, y^{2} + 1 \\le x$.\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF $f_{Y}(y)$." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186176364 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+1\\le x\\}.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a75ec87c30ea5708201cb76816ea3a806648cec197195994cafbe9d9cf588d1.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $-\\infty <y<\\infty $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{{y}^{2}+1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi {x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{{y}^{2}+1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{{y}^{2}+1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{x={y}^{2}+1}^{x=b} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[0-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi ({y}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{\\pi ({y}^{2}+1)}.\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186177001 1064 "We've seen how to construct the marginal mass functions for two discrete random variables from their joint probability mass function. We now wish to formulate analogous definitions when our random variables are continuous.\n\nFirst, recall that if $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of the *discrete* random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively, then\n\n- the marginal mass function of $X$ is given by\n$f_X(x) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y)$\n- and the marginal mass function of $Y$ is given by\n$f_Y(y) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y)$.\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are *continuous* random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y)$.\n\n- The **marginal density function** of $X$ is defined as\n$f_X(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y$.\n- The **marginal density function** of $Y$ is defined as\n$f_Y(y) = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f (x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x$.\n\nWe sometimes refer to a marginal density function as a **marginal probability density function** (or **marginal PDF**).\n\nIt's helpful to appreciate the analogy between the discrete and continuous cases as this often helps us to remember their definitions. In summary:\n\n- To convert from discrete to continuous, we turn sums into integrals.\n- When we compute $f_X$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $Y$. In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $y$.\n- When we compute $f_Y$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $X$. In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $x$.\n\nLet's now see a concrete example of how to compute a marginal density function." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186177001 1064 "We've seen how to construct the marginal mass functions for two discrete random variables from their joint probability mass function. We now wish to formulate analogous definitions when our random variables are continuous. \n\nFirst, recall that if $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of the *discrete* random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively, then\n\n- the marginal mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_X(x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y)\n$$\n\n- and the marginal mass function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are *continuous* random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y).$  \n\n- The **marginal density function** of $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n- The **marginal density function** of $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f (x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to a marginal density function as a **marginal probability density function** (or **marginal PDF**).\n\nIt's helpful to appreciate the analogy between the discrete and continuous cases as this often helps us to remember their definitions. In summary:\n\n- To convert from discrete to continuous, we turn sums into integrals.\n\n- When we compute $f_X$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $Y.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $y.$\n\n- When we compute $f_Y$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $X.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $x.$\n\nLet's now see a concrete example of how to compute a marginal density function." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186177056 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/e2/e229c78e51036e7fb7b7e9345bbe4f2ede5704032d65d2581b53c8a5f59ef603.svg)\n\nLet's find the expression for the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ of $X.$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\leq x \\leq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}]-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_X(x)$ is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e8eb5c2dedf51c1b232af9e2dd6a4eb12621daaa78ab34f18a46e26a9a6d2887.svg)" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186177056 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\n{\\frac{3}{2}(x^{2} + y^{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's find the expression for the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ of $X$.\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{X}(x) &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}(x^{2} + y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} + y^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}[x^{2}y + \\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}[x^{2} \\cdot 1 + \\frac{1^{3}}{3}] - \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot [0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}[x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_X(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}(3x^{2} + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186177057 1064 "Recall that the cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nNow suppose we know the marginal distributions $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ of the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$.\n\nThen we can define the **marginal cumulative distribution functions** (or **marginal CDFs**) of $X$ and $Y$ analogously as follows:\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X$, denoted $F_X(x)$, is given by\n$F_X(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,\\textrm d t$.\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$, denoted $F_Y(y)$, is given by\n$F_Y(y) = P(Y\\leq y) = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^y f_Y(t)\\,\\textrm d t$.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186177057 1064 "Recall that the cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we know the marginal distributions $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ of the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\nThen we can define the **marginal cumulative distribution functions** (or **marginal CDFs**) of $X$ and $Y$ analogously as follows:\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X,$ denoted $F_X(x),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y,$ denoted $F_Y(y),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = P(Y\\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y f_Y(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186247173 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186247173 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 false] [17592186247173 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}